<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="1059" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110163" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16.jpeg 1378w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-238x300.jpeg 238w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-812x1024.jpeg 812w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-768x969.jpeg 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-1218x1536.jpeg 1218w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>A few days ago, <a screenshot=true href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/2025/01/11/wordpress-statistics-me/">I posted about writing a new WordPress stats thing</a> because the Jetpack stats layout annoyed me too much.</p>
<p>I&#8217;ve continued to tinker with it, and I think it should now basically be usable.  I renamed it to <tt>wse</tt> (WordPress Statistics for Emacs), and it&#8217;s on <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/wse" data-cached-time="2025-01-16T07:52:28" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/ewp62mneF.png" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">Microsoft Github</a> if anybody&#8217;s interested in trying it out.</p>
<p>The Emacs portion updates asynchronously in the background now.</p>
<p>I added one thing that doesn&#8217;t have anything to do with statistics per se &#8212; it now also displays recent comments, because that&#8217;s something one has to keep track of (if for no other reason than to delete any spam that slips through the (very good, but not perfect) Akismet anti-spam filter).</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-1.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-1.jpeg" alt="" width="478" height="511" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110164" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-1.jpeg 478w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-1-281x300.jpeg 281w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 478px) 100vw, 478px" /></a></p>
<p>One thing that I&#8217;ve totally missed is that in 2020, Chrome changed how they do inter-site Referrers: They only send over the domain name, but leave off the path part.  I thought I had a bug in my code, or that almost all domains had installed &#8220;referrer scrubbers&#8221; (like Google has always had).  But nope: All you get is the domain name.</p>
<p>This is, of course, good for privacy.  And I&#8217;m totally sure that Google did this Chrome change out of the goodness of their hearts, and not because this forces marketers to sign up for Google-based user tracking even harder than before.  Good on &#8217;em!</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-2.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-2.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="215" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110165" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-2.jpeg 1328w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-2-300x77.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-2-1024x262.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-16-2-768x197.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Speaking of browsers&#8230;  I couldn&#8217;t help adding this little table.  It&#8217;s not that it&#8217;s useful, but I was just kinda sorta curious about what people use these days.  And the audience for this blog is just 50% Chrome users, which is unusual, of course.  Such a discerning audience!</p>
<p>The desktop to mobile ratio surprises me, though &#8212; I thought that would be more 50/50, but nope.  And, of course, tablets have stopped being a thing, but that&#8217;s not really surprising.</p>
<p>The sample is very small (one day), though, so drawing any conclusions here wouldn&#8217;t be a good idea.</p>
